# Platform process definitions (Heroku/Render-style; these platforms run the
# command through a real shell so $PORT expands). NOTE: Railway uses the
# Dockerfile (see railway.json builder=DOCKERFILE), so it does NOT read this
# Procfile — the Dockerfile CMD is the source of truth there.
#
# `release` runs once per deploy before `web` starts taking traffic.
release: python manage.py migrate --noinput && python manage.py collectstatic --noinput
# Hardcoded worker count: some platform expanders do not support the bash
# `${VAR:-default}` syntax, which would pass the literal string to gunicorn.
web: gunicorn config.wsgi:application --bind 0.0.0.0:$PORT --workers 2 --timeout 120 --access-logfile - --error-logfile -

# Background processing (SCALE-READY MODE ONLY).
# These are OPTIONAL and only needed once ENABLE_BACKGROUND_JOBS=true and a
# REDIS_URL/CELERY_BROKER_URL is configured. In lean mode you can run web alone
# and either (a) leave tasks running eagerly inline, and/or (b) trigger the
# scheduled commands from the platform's cron instead of Celery Beat.
#
# worker: consumes the named queues. Scale by running more worker dynos or
#         raising --concurrency. A dedicated OCR worker can be split out with
#         `-Q scanner` so heavy OCR never starves API-facing queues.
worker: celery -A config worker -l info -Q critical,email,notifications,push,scanner,files,billing,analytics,default --concurrency ${CELERY_CONCURRENCY:-2}

# beat: the scheduler (due notifications, trash purge, billing sync, analytics
#       rollups). Run EXACTLY ONE beat process. Skip this and use platform cron
#       instead if you prefer (see docs/deployment/scale-ready-lean-foundation.md).
beat: celery -A config beat -l info
